            "id": "27722182",
            "user": "Caitlynn L",
            "timestamp": "20:08, 12 February 2020",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(9,432 bytes)",
            "change": 335,
            "summary": "(minor fact checking edit for wikiHaus)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "",
                    "== Warnings ==",
                    "*If your home contains insulation manufactured between 1930 and 1950, you should proceed with caution since asbestos could be present. [[Test for Asbestos|Test for asbestos]] and call a professional to remove the insulation if the test is positive.<ref>https://atticareusa.com/blog/tips-attic-insulation-removal/</ref>"
                ],
                "removed": []
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "26861799",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "12:59, 31 July 2019",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(9,097 bytes)",
            "change": 478,
            "summary": "(Added sources for the wikihaus content team.)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "sourcing",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Make sure the attic has a full-size staircase. Building code also requires that the prospective attic living space be accessible by a  full-size staircase with at least 6-foot 8-inch clearance above it. Attics only accessible by trapdoor style staircases or ladders aren't technically \"finishable.\"<ref>https://www.bobvila.com/articles/attic-conversion/#.UyoDEP3B7Kw</ref>  [[Image:1537145 2.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Make certain the attic has two exits. To satisfy fire codes, the attic must have an alternative exist in the event of an emergency. An easily accessible window counts, or some other variety of exit from the attic.<ref>https://www.houselogic.com/by-room/basement-attic/evaluate-your-house-attic-bedroom/</ref>  [[Image:1537145 3.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Get the proper home building permit in your area. To make sure your project will be legal and up to code, you'll need to schedule an inspection at some point when the project is finished and obtain the necessary building permits from the city. Generally, these will be available from the Housing Commission or the City Planning department.<ref>https://www.houselogic.com/by-room/basement-attic/evaluate-your-house-attic-bedroom/</ref>  [[Image:1537145 5.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Remove any existing loose or blown insulation in the attic. Use a builder's vacuum to get all of the small pieces cleaned out of the area. You might consider renting a commercial air purifier for the duration of the project to minimize the possibility of inhaling the tiny insulation strands, which can be hazardous.<ref>https://atticareusa.com/blog/tips-attic-insulation-removal/</ref> [[Image:1537145 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#[[Install Drywall|Hang drywall over the wall insulation]].<ref>https://www.budgetdumpster.com/blog/attic-room-conversion/</ref>  Hang drywall in sheet-sized sections of {{convert|4|ft|m|1}} by {{convert|8|ft|m|sigfig=2}} (121.92 cm by 243.84 cm) with drywall screws at 8 inch (20.32 cm) intervals. Cut smaller drywall pieces with a razor knife to fill in the remaining areas.[[Image:Finish an Attic Step 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#[[Paint a Room|Prime and paint the walls]]. It may take 2 to 3 coats to cover the new drywall.<ref>https://www.budgetdumpster.com/blog/attic-room-conversion/</ref>  Before you put up wallpaper or decorate the walls in any other way, it's important to put down a primer coat to create an even surface amenable to your project.[[Image:Finish an Attic Step 7.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Make sure the attic has a full-size staircase. Building code also requires that the prospective attic living space be accessible by a  full-size staircase with at least 6-foot 8-inch clearance above it. Attics only accessible by trapdoor style staircases or ladders aren't technically \"finishable.\" [[Image:1537145 2.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Make certain the attic has two exits. To satisfy fire codes, the attic must have an alternative exist in the event of an emergency. An easily accessible window counts, or some other variety of exit from the attic. [[Image:1537145 3.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Get the proper home building permit in your area. To make sure your project will be legal and up to code, you'll need to schedule an inspection at some point when the project is finished and obtain the necessary building permits from the city. Generally, these will be available from the Housing Commission or the City Planning department. [[Image:1537145 5.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Remove any existing loose or blown insulation in the attic. Use a builder's vacuum to get all of the small pieces cleaned out of the area. You might consider renting a commercial air purifier for the duration of the project to minimize the possibility of inhaling the tiny insulation strands, which can be hazardous.[[Image:1537145 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#[[Install Drywall|Hang drywall over the wall insulation]]. Hang drywall in sheet-sized sections of {{convert|4|ft|m|1}} by {{convert|8|ft|m|sigfig=2}} (121.92 cm by 243.84 cm) with drywall screws at 8 inch (20.32 cm) intervals. Cut smaller drywall pieces with a razor knife to fill in the remaining areas.[[Image:Finish an Attic Step 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#[[Paint a Room|Prime and paint the walls]]. It may take 2 to 3 coats to cover the new drywall. Before you put up wallpaper or decorate the walls in any other way, it's important to put down a primer coat to create an even surface amenable to your project.[[Image:Finish an Attic Step 7.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
