        {
            "id": "18099809",
            "user": "MiscBot",
            "timestamp": "10:07, 29 July 2015",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(8,544 bytes)",
            "change": -112,
            "summary": "(Standardizing some image attributes that have now been built into images automatically; removing hidden intro images)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Remove any existing loose or blown insulation in the attic. Use a builder's vacuum to get all of the small pieces cleaned out of the area. You might consider renting a commercial air purifier for the duration of the project to minimize the possibility of inhaling the tiny insulation strands, which can be hazardous.[[Image:1537145 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Lay a sub-floor, if necessary. Most attics will need a sub-floor to be laid that you can build on top of. Cut sub-floor panels to the appropriate size and install them over the joist beams. Screw down into joists at 3 foot (91.44 cm) intervals using wood screws.[[Image:Finish an Attic Step 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#[[Install an Electrical Outlet from Scratch|Wire the attic for electricity]]. Install the electrical wiring across the ceiling to the designated spots where the light fixtures will be hung, leaving an 8 inch (20.32 cm) wire hanging (called a lighting pigtail) to connect the light fixtures. Run the electrical wire to the electrical outlets. As a rule of thumb there should be at least 1 electrical outlet every 10 feet (3.05 m).[[Image:Finish an Attic Step 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#[[Insulate Walls|Install new rolled insulation between the wall studs]]. Cut each strip, or batt, to the exact length of the cavity. Press them in place and staple through the paper to the studs. Install the rolled insulation in the floor by cutting to size, pressing into the space between joists and attaching with wire fasteners. If you need to use 2 or more batts to fill in a cavity, be certain that they butt up against each other firmly.[[Image:Finish an Attic Step 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#[[Install Drywall|Hang drywall over the wall insulation]]. Hang drywall in sheet-sized sections of {{convert|4|ft|m|1}} by 8 feet (121.92 cm by 243.84 cm) with drywall screws at 8 inch (20.32 cm) intervals. Cut smaller drywall pieces with a razor knife to fill in the remaining areas.[[Image:Finish an Attic Step 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#[[Paint a Room|Prime and paint the walls]]. It may take 2 to 3 coats to cover the new drywall. Before you put up wallpaper or decorate the walls in any other way, it's important to put down a primer coat to create an even surface amenable to your project.[[Image:Finish an Attic Step 7.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Install the flooring. In attics, carpeting is popular to cut down on the noise of upstairs traffic. If you want to lay hardwood floors, consider putting down some substantial rugs both to cut the noise component and to keep the room feeling comfortable.<ref>http://www.thisoldhouse.com/toh/photos/0,,20533380_21062308,00.html</ref>[[Image:Finish an Attic Step 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Trim the room and decorate. Install base boards, light fixtures, electrical outlets and switch plate covers. Decorate the room to your taste. Attic space makes for a popular: [[Image:Finish an Attic Step 9.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Remove any existing loose or blown insulation in the attic. Use a builder's vacuum to get all of the small pieces cleaned out of the area. You might consider renting a commercial air purifier for the duration of the project to minimize the possibility of inhaling the tiny insulation strands, which can be hazardous.<br><br>[[Image:1537145 6.jpg|center|416px]]",
                    "#Lay a sub-floor, if necessary. Most attics will need a sub-floor to be laid that you can build on top of. Cut sub-floor panels to the appropriate size and install them over the joist beams. Screw down into joists at 3 foot (91.44 cm) intervals using wood screws.<br><br>[[Image:Finish an Attic Step 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#[[Install an Electrical Outlet from Scratch|Wire the attic for electricity]]. Install the electrical wiring across the ceiling to the designated spots where the light fixtures will be hung, leaving an 8 inch (20.32 cm) wire hanging (called a lighting pigtail) to connect the light fixtures. Run the electrical wire to the electrical outlets. As a rule of thumb there should be at least 1 electrical outlet every 10 feet (3.05 m).<br><br>[[Image:Finish an Attic Step 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#[[Insulate Walls|Install new rolled insulation between the wall studs]]. Cut each strip, or batt, to the exact length of the cavity. Press them in place and staple through the paper to the studs. Install the rolled insulation in the floor by cutting to size, pressing into the space between joists and attaching with wire fasteners. If you need to use 2 or more batts to fill in a cavity, be certain that they butt up against each other firmly.<br><br>[[Image:Finish an Attic Step 5.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#[[Install Drywall|Hang drywall over the wall insulation]]. Hang drywall in sheet-sized sections of {{convert|4|ft|m|1}} by 8 feet (121.92 cm by 243.84 cm) with drywall screws at 8 inch (20.32 cm) intervals. Cut smaller drywall pieces with a razor knife to fill in the remaining areas.<br><br>[[Image:Finish an Attic Step 6.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#[[Paint a Room|Prime and paint the walls]]. It may take 2 to 3 coats to cover the new drywall. Before you put up wallpaper or decorate the walls in any other way, it's important to put down a primer coat to create an even surface amenable to your project.<br><br>[[Image:Finish an Attic Step 7.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Install the flooring. In attics, carpeting is popular to cut down on the noise of upstairs traffic. If you want to lay hardwood floors, consider putting down some substantial rugs both to cut the noise component and to keep the room feeling comfortable.<ref>http://www.thisoldhouse.com/toh/photos/0,,20533380_21062308,00.html</ref><br><br>[[Image:Finish an Attic Step 8.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Trim the room and decorate. Install base boards, light fixtures, electrical outlets and switch plate covers. Decorate the room to your taste. Attic space makes for a popular: <br><br>[[Image:Finish an Attic Step 9.jpg|center|550px]]"
                ]
            }
        },
